0 // DERIVED FROM THE OFFICIAL LDRAW LIBRARY, MECHANICALLY AND VERIFIABLY.
0 // 3003.dat, s\3003s01.dat and s\3003s02.dat (James Jessiman, Steffen,
0 // Owen Burgoyne; CC BY 4.0, library updates 2022-05 / 2013-02) flattened in
0 // reference order, with the ONE interior `stud4.dat` line removed. Every
0 // other line is the library's own geometry, byte for byte. A one-line diff
0 // against the flattened originals is the whole derivation.
0 //
0 // WHY: a Kiddicraft Self-Locking brick and a 1949 Automatic Binding Brick
0 // have no tube inside. The tube is the 1958 patent and it is what made the
0 // clutch hold. Building a 1924, 1940 or 1949 brick from a modern 3001/3003
0 // puts a 1958 feature under a pre-1958 caption -- which is exactly the
0 // claim Act III makes, so it cannot be a stand-in there. External
0 // dimensions are deliberately untouched: the Brighton Toy Museum's own
0 // comparison of a ~1947 Kiddicraft against a 2012 LEGO brick finds the
0 // difference "undetectable with a micrometer". What differs is the slots,
0 // the rounded vertical edges, the bobbled tops and the hollow underside.
0 //
0 // STILL MISSING: Hilary Page's END SLOTS in the walls, and the rounded
0 // edges and bobbled tops. Those are the visible signature and they want
0 // real geometry rather than a subtraction.
0 //
0 // NOT YET USED BY THE SHOW. `spex show-build` reads a scene as a list of
0 // PART INSTANCES: loose polygons in a scene file are dropped, and a scene's
0 // references are resolved against the LDraw library only, never against the
0 // scene's own directory. So this file cannot be reached yet from
0 // ldraw-scenes/*.ldr. Real LDraw resolvers search the model directory
0 // first; giving spex the same search is what makes Act III's three bricks
0 // buildable, and it is a change to spex-ldraw rather than to a document.
0 //
0 // LDraw is a trademark of the LDraw Organization. Part geometry used under
0 // the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 licence.

